NAME
       cut - print selected fields or characters from each line

SYNOPSIS
       cut -d DELIM -f LIST [-s] [FILE...]
       cut -c LIST [FILE...]
       cut -b LIST [FILE...]

DESCRIPTION
       cut reads each line of FILE (or standard input) and prints only the
       parts you choose: fields separated by a delimiter (-f with -d), or
       character positions (-c). Use it to pull one column out of files like
       /etc/passwd, or out of a command's output.

       A LIST is one or more numbers or ranges, separated by commas:

       3          the third field
       1,6        fields 1 and 6
       2-4        fields 2 to 4
       5-         field 5 to the end
       -3         fields 1 to 3

       Fields are printed in file order, whatever order the list has.

OPTIONS
       -d CHAR, --delimiter=CHAR
              The field separator (one character). The default is a tab.
              cut does not treat several spaces as one; for space-aligned
              output use awk '{print $2}' instead.

       -f LIST, --fields=LIST
              Print these fields.

       -c LIST, --characters=LIST
              Print these character positions.

       -b LIST, --bytes=LIST
              Print these byte positions.

       -s, --only-delimited
              Skip lines that have no delimiter at all.

       --complement
              Print everything except the selected fields.

       --output-delimiter=TEXT
              Join the printed fields with TEXT instead of the input
              delimiter.

EXAMPLES
       List all user names:

           cut -d: -f1 /etc/passwd

       Show each user's name and login shell:

           cut -d: -f1,7 /etc/passwd

       The first three characters of each line:

           cut -c1-3 /root/words.txt

       Save the names of users whose shell is bash:

           grep 'bash$' /etc/passwd | cut -d: -f1 > /root/bashusers.txt

SEE ALSO
       awk(1), paste(1), column(1), sort(1)
